00:09:08.278 --> 00:09:15.218
(Cheers and applause)

00:09:18.388 --> 00:09:20.390
Hello everybody, my name is Jacob Collier,

00:09:20.390 --> 00:09:22.492
and I am this year's
music curator for TED.

00:09:22.526 --> 00:09:24.661
And the musician
you just heard, Wallis Bird,

00:09:24.695 --> 00:09:26.630
is one of my dearest musical companions.

00:09:26.630 --> 00:09:27.798
She is crazy.

00:09:27.831 --> 00:09:30.934
I don't know how you felt,
but the first time I heard Wallis sing,

00:09:30.968 --> 00:09:32.269
I had tears and goosebumps.

00:09:32.269 --> 00:09:33.971
She's like a roof-raising musician.

00:09:33.971 --> 00:09:37.407
She accompanied me on tour, opening
for some shows a couple of years ago,

00:09:37.441 --> 00:09:38.976
and I knew then what I know now,

00:09:39.009 --> 00:09:42.579
which is that she possesses that rare
gift that not many musicians possess,

00:09:42.579 --> 00:09:46.016
which is the ability to win over
any crowd with her totally wild energy,

00:09:46.049 --> 00:09:47.684
her joy, and her colors.

00:09:47.718 --> 00:09:51.154
Her voice to me is like this chameleon.

00:09:51.188 --> 00:09:53.857
It can change color,
it can change shape, it's stretchy.

00:09:53.857 --> 00:09:56.259
It's this magnificent instrument of joy.

00:09:56.293 --> 00:09:59.563
And I think one of my favorite moments
of the talk you just witnessed

00:09:59.563 --> 00:10:02.399
was that moment where she goes
higher and higher and higher,

00:10:02.399 --> 00:10:04.735
and the energy just builds
and builds and builds.

00:10:04.735 --> 00:10:06.703
She really is unlike any musician I know.

00:10:06.737 --> 00:10:09.106
Hope you enjoyed.
Lots of love, cheerio, bye.

